{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011UNR3EYwr9y7HEb2f5C862","type":"tool_result","content":"226:\\section{Conclusion}\n\\section{Conclusion}\nFor connected graphs with at most $30$ vertices, a ridge regression on five relabeling-invariant log-statistics predicted Monte Carlo start-averaged cover time with a family-macro log MAE of $0.181$ in a sealed evaluation, compared with $1.754$ for the generic $2m(n-1)$ bound, $0.870$ for a constant predictor, and $1.423$ when the predictions were scored against shuffled targets. Median relative Monte Carlo label error was $0.029$, small compared with the gap. The evaluation completed with a qualified outcome. In answer to the first research question, cheap statistics can give a markedly more accurate point prediction than the bound on this sample; in answer to the second, the retained evidence does not say on which graph types the predictor is reliable or fails. The most valuable next measurements are inexpensive and well-defined: per-family and per-size error reporting with Monte Carlo and graph-bootstrap uncertainty, per-feature ablations, sensitivity to the ridge penalty and training-set composition, and a comparison with richer invariants such as resistance-based features \\citep{Doyle1984RandomWA}.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n['Resistance-calibrated Matthews predictor for small-graph cover time']\n{\"attempt_counts\": {\"scored\": 0, \"total\": 1, \"unscored\": 1}, \"baseline_program_dir\": \"/home/user/orca/projects/researchworld-eval/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-s00-20261027T171535Z/work/results/s00/\n[]\n{'forbidden_visible_terms': ['eligible', 'correct and eligible', 'correctness rate', 'worker_01', 'worker_02', 'combined_score', 'rubric'], 'internal_fields': ['correct', 'eligible'], 'preferred_terms': {'idea': 'model variant', 'ideas': 'model variants', 'implementation': 'configuration', 'implementations': 'configurations', 'program': 'seeded run', 'programs': 'seeded runs'}, 'run_unit': 'seeded_run', 'threshold_rate_rule': 'If a thresholded rate appears in the visible paper, keep one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse a single label for different task-specific thresholds.', 'variant_unit': 'model_variant'}\n70:Let $G=(V,E)$ be a connected, undirected graph with $n=|V|$ and $m=|E|$. Let $C_v(G)$ be the expected number of steps for a simple random walk started at $v$ to visit all vertices, and let $C(G)=\\frac1n\\sum_{v}C_v(G)$ be the start-averaged cover time, which is the prediction target. A predictor receives a set of labelled training graphs (edges, a Monte Carlo estimate of $C(G)$ and its standard error) and unlabeled test graphs, and must return a finite positive prediction for every test graph. Vertex numbering and edge order carry no information: the evaluation sends randomly relabeled, edge-shuffled copies of the test graphs and requires predictions to agree to a relative tolerance of $10^{-6}$. % @evidence[[protocol_facts.json#PERM_TOL]]\n77:Why should a few statistics help? The ratio between $2m(n-1)$ and the true cover time depends strongly on structure, and exactly solvable families make this concrete. Writing $H_k$ for the $k$-th harmonic number and $N=n-1$, the start-averaged cover times are: $C=N^2+N(N-1)/6$ on the path (with $m=N$); $C=n(n-1)/2$ on the cycle ($m=n$); $C=(n-1)H_{n-1}$ on the complete graph (a coupon-collector argument, $m=n(n-1)/2$); and $C=2NH_{N-1}+1/(N+1)$ on the star ($m=N$). We checked these closed forms against exact subset-dynamic-programming enumeration at $n=7$ (\\texttt{scripts/verify\\_closed\\_forms.py}). % @evidence[[scripts/verify_closed_forms.py]]\n78:Figure~\\ref{fig:gap} plots the resulting log overshoot $\\ln[2m(n-1)/C]$. On the path it is essentially constant, about $0.54$ at $n=30$; on the cycle it is exactly $\\ln 4\\approx1.39$ at every $n$; on the star it grows like $\\ln(n/\\ln n)$ to about $2.0$ at $n=30$; and on the complete graph it grows like $\\ln(n^2/\\ln n)$ to about $5.4$ at $n=30$. % @evidence[[closed_form_gap.json#log_overshoot]]\n115:\\paragraph{Graphs and labels.} Graphs are synthesised by code from twelve families: path, cycle, star, complete, grid, complete bipartite, barbell, lollipop, binary tree, random tree, Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi, and random regular. The sealed evaluation uses $36$ labelled training graphs ($3$ per family, $6\\le n\\le 30$) and $24$ test graphs ($2$ per family, $8\\le n\\le 26$). % @evidence[[protocol_facts.json#final_train]] @evidence[[protocol_facts.json#final_test]]\n116:Test graphs are content-hash disjoint from the graphs visible during model selection. Labels are Monte Carlo estimates of $C(G)$ computed by the evaluator from simple-random-walk simulations started equally often from every vertex, with about $300$ walks per graph in total; each label comes with a standard error. % @evidence[[protocol_facts.json#MC_WALKS]]\n119:\\paragraph{Episodes and seeds.} The predictor is evaluated in three seeded episodes (seeds $201$, $202$, $203$); each episode resets all state, supplies the labelled graphs in batches of at most $16$, and requests predictions for the test graphs. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n131:Table~\\ref{tab:main} and Figure~\\ref{fig:main} report the sealed-evaluation scores. The five-statistic ridge predictor attains a family-macro log MAE of $0.181$ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.macro_log_mae]]\n132:whereas the $2m(n-1)$ bound attains $1.754$, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.bound_macro_log_mae]]\n133:a difference of $1.572$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.improvement_vs_bound]]\n134:Exponentiating the family-macro log MAE gives a rough geometric-average multiplicative error factor of $1.20$ for the predictor and $5.78$ for the bound; this conversion is arithmetic on the family-macro mean and is not a per-graph guarantee. The constant predictor scores $0.870$ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.const_macro_log_mae]]\n135:and the shuffled-target control $1.423$, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.shuffle_macro_log_mae]]\n154:Five-statistic ridge & $0.181$ & $1.20$ & $1.0$ \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.macro_log_mae]]\n155:Constant (training geometric mean) & $0.870$ & $2.39$ & $4.8$ \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.const_macro_log_mae]]\n156:Ridge scored vs.\\ shuffled targets & $1.423$ & $4.15$ & $7.8$ \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.shuffle_macro_log_mae]]\n157:Bound $2m(n-1)$ & $1.754$ & $5.78$ & $9.7$ \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.bound_macro_log_mae]]\n167:\\paragraph{Invariance.} The maximum relative deviation between predictions on a test graph and on its relabeled, edge-shuffled copies is $0.0$, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.perm_max_rel_dev]]\n171:The labels are Monte Carlo estimates. The median relative standard error over test graphs is $0.029$, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mc_rel_se_median]]\n172:about $2.9\\%$, or roughly one sixth of the ridge log MAE ($0.029/0.181\\approx0.16$; arithmetic). The label noise is thus small relative to the prediction error and very small relative to the bound's error, and the headline gap cannot be explained by label noise. A simple calibration helps: if the predictor were exact and each label were perturbed by a Gaussian relative error with standard deviation equal to this median, the expected absolute log error would be $\\sqrt{2/\\pi}\\times0.029\\approx0.023$, about $13\\%$ of the observed $0.181$. % @evidence[[closed_form_gap.json#noise_floor_mae_gaussian]]\n176:The three seeded episodes each yield the same score, $-0.18137$ (the sign convention is that the score is the negated family-macro log MAE), with durations of roughly $0.06$ seconds each. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n180:The sealed evaluation completed and its outcome is recorded as qualified. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\n184:The second research question asks how error depends on graph type. The retained evaluation record reports only family-macro aggregates; per-family and per-size errors were not retained, and we do not attempt to reconstruct them. We can therefore not say that the predictor is reliable on, or fails on, any named family. The aggregation itself offers a bounded statement: since the score is an unweighted mean over $12$ families, a macro value of $0.181$ means the average family-level mean log error is $0.181$, and this is compatible with a mix of families with small errors and a few with larger ones. Which of these holds remains unknown. Arithmetic does give a loose bound: since errors are non-negative, no family mean can exceed $12\\times0.181\\approx2.18$ (a multiplicative factor of about $8.8$), and, with two test graphs per family, no single graph's log error can exceed $24\\times0.181\\approx4.35$ (a factor of about $78$). % @evidence[[closed_form_gap.json#worst_case_family_mean_log_error]]\n240:The studied configuration is a single Python module; the table lists the elements that determine its predictions and where they are defined in the inspected source (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}). % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py]]\n265:Table~\\ref{tab:splits} gives the composition of the graph sets used by the sealed evaluation and, for context, by the selection procedure. % @evidence[[protocol_facts.json#final_train]] @evidence[[protocol_facts.json#search_train]]\n285:Aggregation is fixed before evaluation with equal weights: per-graph error $\\min(|\\ln(\\hat y/y)|,10)$, mean within family, unweighted mean over families, mean over episodes. A failed episode would contribute $10$ to the macro error and a perturbation deviation of $10^{6}$; none is recorded here. Monte Carlo labels simulate walks from every start vertex equally often, about $300$ walks per graph in total (run-time \\texttt{MC\\_WALKS}$=300$), with the standard error computed from the sample standard deviation of observed cover times. % @evidence[[protocol_facts.json#MC_WALKS]] @evidence[[protocol_facts.json#ERR_CLIP]]\n297:201 & $-0.18137$ & $0.058$ \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n304:The evaluation status is completed and the qualification outcome is qualified. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\n308:The configuration studied here is the reference configuration retained by model selection. The selection procedure considered one alternative candidate, a resistance-calibrated Matthews-style predictor, and it produced no score: zero candidates were scored and one was unscored. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\n309:Unscored candidates are missing evidence rather than measured losses, and we draw no conclusion about that alternative. The selection score of the retained configuration was $-0.1913$ (a negated macro log MAE on the selection test graphs), identical to the reference by construction, so the score difference is $0.0$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.baseline_score]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.search_score_delta]]\n313:The predictor needs only a Python 3 interpreter with the standard library. The evaluation harness supplies the Monte Carlo labels and seeds; the reported seeds are $201$, $202$ and $203$. The scripts \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} and \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_protocol.py} regenerate Figure~\\ref{fig:main} and the protocol facts used in this paper from the evaluation record and the task resources; the former requires matplotlib. % @evidence[[scripts/build_figures.py]] @evidence[[scripts/extract_protocol.py]]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"12cfe8e1-3f02-4854-9a60-0123e40e0786","uuid":"eac1c2a9-85de-48c9-a946-8b5c7c5c461d","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:58:22.498Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"226:\\section{Conclusion}\n\\section{Conclusion}\nFor connected graphs with at most $30$ vertices, a ridge regression on five relabeling-invariant log-statistics predicted Monte Carlo start-averaged cover time with a family-macro log MAE of $0.181$ in a sealed evaluation, compared with $1.754$ for the generic $2m(n-1)$ bound, $0.870$ for a constant predictor, and $1.423$ when the predictions were scored against shuffled targets. Median relative Monte Carlo label error was $0.029$, small compared with the gap. The evaluation completed with a qualified outcome. In answer to the first research question, cheap statistics can give a markedly more accurate point prediction than the bound on this sample; in answer to the second, the retained evidence does not say on which graph types the predictor is reliable or fails. The most valuable next measurements are inexpensive and well-defined: per-family and per-size error reporting with Monte Carlo and graph-bootstrap uncertainty, per-feature ablations, sensitivity to the ridge penalty and training-set composition, and a comparison with richer invariants such as resistance-based features \\citep{Doyle1984RandomWA}.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n['Resistance-calibrated Matthews predictor for small-graph cover time']\n{\"attempt_counts\": {\"scored\": 0, \"total\": 1, \"unscored\": 1